omx-panel (0.4.3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A control name holding a line break, a return or a tab no longer writes
    invalid Turtle: lilv refused the whole bundle (#51).
  * The DEB of 0.4.2 was not published (lintian refused the unit's WantedBy
    plugin-qualify-sync.service); it is overridden with its reason, and this is
    the first DEB with omx-modgui-sync's search rule and a unit that follows
    plugin-qualify-sync.

 -- Pau Aliagas <linuxnow@gmail.com>  Wed, 28 Oct 2026 10:00:00 +0000

omx-panel (0.4.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * omx-modgui-sync reads the LV2 search list as plugin-hostd and plugin-qualify
    do ($LV2_PATH, else plugin-hostd's default), writes its bundles to
    /usr/local/lib/lv2 (~/.lv2 for a user; the folder may hold plugins too, and
    the bundles are named omx-modgui-<name>.lv2), and has --check: it says
    whether mod-host and mod-ui read the folder, and lists the CLAP search
    list (#44).
  * The unit runs after plugin-qualify-sync once enabled, under CPU, memory and
    task limits. The Debian-only apt hook is gone.

 -- Pau Aliagas <linuxnow@gmail.com>  Wed, 28 Oct 2026 08:00:00 +0000

omx-panel (0.4.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The DEB builds on Debian bookworm again: python3-lilv is no build
    dependency (bookworm has none); the omx-modgui-sync tests skip without it.
    0.4.0 was published for the RPM only (#44).

 -- Pau Aliagas <linuxnow@gmail.com>  Tue, 27 Oct 2026 23:30:00 +0000

omx-panel (0.4.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * omx-modgui-sync, moved here from the Zynthian image: gives a generated MOD
    GUI to every LV2 plugin that ships none, into /var/lib/omx/modgui
    (standard directories, set in /etc/omx/modgui-sync.conf). The unit is
    installed, not enabled; the apt hook starts it only when it is enabled. A
    plugin that cannot get a GUI never fails the run (#44).

 -- Pau Aliagas <linuxnow@gmail.com>  Tue, 27 Oct 2026 23:00:00 +0000

omx-panel (0.3.4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The unit table is read from the contract data (param-units.json, installed
    by omx-plugins-contract) and not from the C header omx_param_contract.h,
    which only omx-plugins-dev ships: a runtime install lays out a control
    with a unit. omx-plugins-contract (>= 0.15.0) is now a dependency.
    $OMX_PARAM_CONTRACT_H is gone (#44).

 -- Pau Aliagas <linuxnow@gmail.com>  Tue, 27 Oct 2026 22:00:00 +0000

